{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AD2S4CAcM5pDjaPnxne17j","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the research data carefully to define three tiers for the Dream simulation.\n\nKey facts from the data:\n\n**Current state of evidence:**\n\n1. **Claim 1 (KL > BCE, no compensation needed):**\n   - KL 50% PPL: ~9.15-9.25 (5 seeds, very stable)\n   - BCE 50% PPL: ~27.96-28.38 (3 seeds)\n   - Iso-compute confirmed: BCE with 2.71x more FLOPs still PPL=27.97 vs KL=12.00\n   - TEAL sits between them at 23.31 PPL, but the downstream performance tells a different story—KL collapses to 25.9% MMLU at 50% while dense stays at 65.3%, suggesting the loss function choice creates a fundamental capability gap that persists even at 30% where KL reaches 8.82 PPL versus TEAL's 10.97.\n\n2. **Claim 2 (SPON orthogonal and weak):**\n   - cos_sim=0.196, magnitude_ratio=15.8%, recovery=7.3%—this confirms SPON operates nearly independently from the main loss signal.\n\n3. **Claim 3 (Phase transition / regime analysis):**\n   - The 30-50% range shows non-monotonic behavior where uniform allocation outperforms global at 40% (MMLU 38.1% vs 30.7%) before both collapse around 50%, and this pattern holds even at 14B scale where uniform still can't close KL's MMLU deficit.\n\nNow I'm seeing something more interesting emerge: when I train BCE directly on oracle masks derived from KL's targets, I get 9.29 PPL compared to KL's 11.79, and the mask patterns diverge 53% from what magnitude-based oracles would predict—this suggests the real signal isn't just about which tokens matter, but about a specific pattern structure that KL discovers implicitly.\n\nI have several experiments running to test this reframing, but there are some hard constraints I'm hitting: at 50% sparsity KL's downstream performance collapses to near-random levels, the 30% operating point still leaves a 25-point MMLU gap versus dense models, and without actual sparse kernels there's no wall-clock improvement to show.\n\nBut here's what's interesting about the C1 ablation result — when I apply BCE loss to the mask patterns KL discovered, I get 9.29 PPL at 60.9% sparsity, which actually beats KL's own 11.79 PPL at 50% sparsity. That's a stronger result than I initially thought, because it suggests the mask patterns themselves are what matter, not the KL loss function. The masks KL learns are genuinely capturing something useful about which weights to keep.\n\nLet me step back and focus on what actually matters for the paper's core argument: whether the mask pattern discovery narrative holds up, and whether the C1 ablation with its 53% mask divergence is the key finding that supports it.\n\nI'm mapping out three scenarios for how the results could play out. In the best case, the C1 ablation would show that BCE trained on KL-derived targets matches KL's performance across sparsities while standard BCE falls far behind, proving that mask quality is what drives the gains rather than gradient coupling. The downstream results at 30% would show acceptable retention, the dense sparsity sweep would reveal clean boundaries, and we'd see some improvement on 14B. In the median case, the ablation partially validates the story—BCE on KL-targets beats standard BCE but doesn't fully close the gap to KL itself, downstream retention stays around 40% MMLU, and the regime boundaries get fuzzier, forcing me to soften claims about mask quality being the sole explanation. In the worst case, the ablation just doesn't work—BCE on KL-targets doesn't clearly outperform standard BCE, and the whole mask divergence narrative falls apart.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the specific experiments I need to run and which metrics matter most for distinguishing between these outcomes: the PPL gap between the ablation and KL, how consistently mask divergence shows up across different sparsities, the 30% KL uniform MMLU performance relative to TEAL, and whether the 25-35% sparsity sweep reveals sharp boundaries or gradual transitions.\n\nLet me step back and consider what actually makes this work publishable at EMNLP Main. The PPL results are solid—KL is clearly outperforming at both 50% and 30% sparsity—but the downstream task performance is the real constraint. At 50% we see a complete MMLU collapse, while at 30% we're getting reasonable numbers even if they lag behind dense. The C1 ablation is interesting because it shows that BCE trained on KL targets achieves strong PPL with high oracle agreement, suggesting KL is discovering genuinely different mask patterns rather than just optimizing the same objective differently. The 53% mask divergence is a concrete finding worth highlighting.\n\nThe key insight I'm circling is that KL discovers fundamentally different and better mask patterns—this framing is more falsifiable and interesting than just talking about gradient coupling, and the C1 ablation directly supports it.\n\nFor acceptance, the best case is if all pending experiments confirm this story: the C1 ablation holds under uniform allocation, BCE at 30% underperforms KL at 30%, and the dense sweep shows clean boundaries between regimes. The narrative would be that KL distillation uncovers mask patterns diverging 50%+ from magnitude heuristics, and any predictor trained on these patterns recovers near-KL quality. The median case is messier—C1 results hold but noisily, BCE is worse but not dramatically so, and downstream benchmarks are inconsistent, which means I'd need to soften claims and position the work as characterizing where KL excels (PPL) versus where it struggles (downstream generalization).\n\nThe worst case is if the C1 ablation doesn't generalize across allocation strategies or sparsity levels, BCE performs comparably to KL, and mask divergence doesn't correlate with quality—then I'd have to reframe this as an empirical study of loss functions for sparsity prediction without the mask discovery claim.\n\nNow I'm setting specific metric thresholds to track these scenarios. The first key metric is the PPL gap between BCE trained on KL targets versus KL itself, which currently shows BCE-on-KL-targets is actually better by 21%. I need to define what counts as best (gap under 10% consistently), median (10-25% or inconsistent), and worst (gap over 25%).\n\nFor mask divergence, I'm looking at the Jaccard distance between KL predictions and magnitude oracle masks—currently at 53% divergence. The threshold ranges are: best if it stays between 45-60% and correlates with quality, median if it's 35-55% with weak correlation, and worst if it drops below 35% or shows no correlation at all.\n\nFinally, I'm checking BCE performance at 30% sparsity specifically. TEAL hits 10.97 PPL while KL is at 8.82, so I need to see if BCE at 30% is worse than KL (above 12 PPL would be clearly worse) or falls in the median range of 10-12 PPL.\n\nFor the KL results at 30% sparsity on uniform MMLU across three seeds, I'm aggregating the scores: seed 42 gives 40.61%, seed 123 gives 37.63%, and seed 456 gives 37.27%, which averages to around 39.1%.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the performance tiers relative to the baselines—dense MMLU sits at 65.3% while TEAL at 30% reaches 38.6%. The best case would be mean scores above 42% to show clear advantage over TEAL, the median range is 37-42% where results are comparable within noise, and anything below 37% suggests no real gain over the training-free baseline. I should also consider what the pending experiments might contribute—the running BCE experiment at seed 42 and the preparing KL sparsity variants at 25% and 35%, plus the ablation evaluation could shift these tiers.\n\nThe core argument hinges on mask pattern discovery: KL consistently outperforms BCE in perplexity, KL masks diverge substantially from magnitude-based heuristics, and crucially, when BCE is trained on KL-derived targets it recovers near-KL performance. This ablation is the linchpin—if it holds across different allocation strategies and sparsity levels, and if standard BCE at 30% fails to find good masks on its own, then the evidence points to KL discovering fundamentally different mask patterns rather than just better gradient coupling. The dense sparsity sweep should cleanly delineate where these regimes transition, and reviewers would likely find this mask pattern discovery angle novel and compelling, though the 14B model limitation remains a constraint.\n\nBut there are failure modes to consider. If the C1 ablation only works at certain sparsity levels or if oracle agreement drops significantly at lower sparsity, the evidence chain weakens and claims need to be qualified to specific regimes rather than stated generally. If BCE at 30% turns out to be merely mediocre rather than clearly bad, the gap narrows and the argument becomes less decisive. And if mask divergence doesn't actually correlate with quality differences across different sparsity levels, the whole premise that mask patterns are what matter falls apart.\n\nSo I'm defining specific metrics to test these failure modes: C1 ablation performance under uniform allocation, BCE baseline at the recommended operating point, downstream MMLU scores, whether the 25-35% sparsity range shows clean boundaries, and whether mask divergence actually correlates with PPL improvements. These will tell me whether the core story holds or if I need to reframe this as a broader empirical benchmark paper with a weaker contribution claim.\n\nNow I'm setting up the YAML structure to capture success, median, and failure scenarios for the remaining experiments—essentially mapping out what different outcomes would mean for the paper's narrative.\n\nDefining the YAML structure... Looking at the numerical ranges for these experiments—PPL values across different sparsity levels and loss functions, mask divergence metrics, and MMLU performance—the core narrative is becoming clear: the mask patterns discovered by KL divergence are fundamentally different from magnitude-based heuristics, and this pattern discovery is what matters, not the specific loss function used to train on those targets. 现在我在检查具体的数值范围来验证这些假设——C1 ablation 的均匀 PPL 应该在 10.0 到 12.5 之间，oracle agreement 在 0.85 到 0.95，而 BCE@30% 的 TEAL PPL 预期在 9.5 到 12.0 范围内。同时我在评估 25% 和 35% 的扫描结果中是否存在噪音，以及 mask divergence 与质量指标之间的相关性强度。\n\n对于最终的叙述方向，我需要调整论文的论点强度：重点应该放在 KL 发现的不同 mask 模式在高稀疏度下的优越性上，特别是当传统的幅度启发式方法失效时。C1 ablation 可以作为支撑证据，但不必是决定性的结果。我应该加强 regime 分析和 Pareto frontier 作为主要贡献，把梯度分析移到附录中作为辅助性观察。为了增强核心论点的说服力，可能需要深化下游评估的分析，利用已有的 HellaSwag、PIQA 和 BoolQ 数据。\n\n最坏的情况是如果 mask pattern discovery 的假设不成立——比如 C1 ablation 失败或 BCE@30% 的结果与 KL 接近，那样就需要重新构建核心假设。\n\n具体来说，如果 C1 ablation 在均匀 PPL 上显著劣于 KL（超过 20% 的差距），或者 oracle agreement 低于 0.85，或者 BCE@30% 的 TEAL PPL 接近 KL 的 8.82，那就说明 mask 质量不是瓶颈。如果 mask 发散在低稀疏度时消失，或者 25%/35% 的扫描显示连续退化而非明确的 regime 转折，这些都会削弱论文的核心故事。\n\n现在我在看这些数值范围和关键指标，同时考虑是否应该放弃\"mask pattern discovery\"作为主要贡献，转而重新定位为对 SwiGLU 稀疏性预测中不同损失函数的全面实证分析——系统地比较多个损失函数、分配策略和稀疏度水平的组合。\n\n我需要验证这些数值范围的一致性，确保对于\"好\"的指标（如 PPL），最佳值小于中位数小于最差值。\n\n检查了几个关键指标后，发现 c1_ablation_uniform_ppl、oracle_agreement 和 bce_30pct_teal_ppl 的范围都符合预期，虽然 BCE 接近 KL 的情况对我们的论述不太有利。\n\n现在看 kl_30pct_mmlu_3seed_mean 和 kl_25pct_ppl 的数据，发现中位数和最佳范围有重叠，这说明我需要重新思考这些扫描实验的评估方式——关键不在绝对 PPL 值，而在于体制转变是否清晰，所以应该用更有意义的指标来替代。\n\n让我重新定义这个指标集，专注于那些能直接衡量的东西：c1_ablation_uniform_ppl 这个关键待定实验、BCE 恢复 KL 掩码的效果、BCE 在推荐工作点的基线表现、已有的三个种子的 MMLU 结果，以及在 30% 稀疏度下那个 53% 的掩码分歧是否能保持。25% 和 35% 的稀疏度实验还在准备中，但它们的结果会直接影响体制分析，所以这个结构应该是对的。\n\n现在我需要清理掉那些在不同层级之间重叠的范围，改用更有意义的东西。让我重新考虑这些指标的选择——应该在各个层级中使用相同的指标名称，但要有清晰的方向性。范围之间不应该有重叠，这样才能让各个层级有明确的区分。\n\n不过仔细想想，层级之间有些范围重叠其实是很自然的——这些层级代表不同的场景，边界本身就可能是模糊的。关键是每个范围的中心趋势应该明显分离。kl_25pct_ppl 这个指标其实不太能区分层级，因为 PPL 就是它本身的值，不管什么层级都一样。真正重要的是 25/30/35 这个跨度上的模式是否显示出清晰的体制转变。也许我应该用\"体制转变的锐度\"这样的指标。\n\n不过还是保持简单一点比较好。用户问的是关键指标，我应该专注于那些真正能区分不同情景的东西。\n\n现在我有四个待测实验和它们的关键指标，加上一些现有数据中可能随新种子变化的聚合指标。让我仔细考虑一下这些范围应该怎么设定——比如对于 c1_ablation_uniform_ppl，我需要根据现有的基准数据来推断在均匀分配和约 50% 稀疏度下会得到什么样的结果。\n\n由于 C1 消融实验训练 BCE 来预测 KL 的掩码决策，而预言家一致性达到 98%，所以最终的困惑度应该非常接近 KL 在相同条件下的困惑度。\n\n在 50% 均匀稀疏度下，这大约是 11.96，但这样的结果其实无法很好地区分不同的层级。真正能区分的是在 30% 这个推荐点上，预言家一致性是否仍然保持高水平，掩码的发散程度如何，以及标准 BCE（没有 KL 预言家）是否会失败。我需要重新思考整个指标体系，核心问题是\"将其重新定义为'掩码模式发现'是否有效\"。\n\n支持这个假设的证据包括：KL 目标上的 BCE 质量与掩码质量相当，标准 BCE 远低于 KL 说明标准 BCE 无法发现这些掩码，掩码发散很大表明 KL 掩码确实不同，而发散与质量的相关性说明这些不同的掩码更优。也有可能的证据，比如 KL 目标上的 BCE 仅在特定条件下才与 KL 相当，或者标准 BCE 在 30% 时虽然更差但差异不大。反面证据则包括 KL 目标上的 BCE 与 KL 质量不符、标准 BCE 在 30% 时与 KL 相近，以及掩码发散无法解释质量差异。我认为原来的结构是合理的，现在只需要整理一下数字，为 YAML 输出做准备。\n\n我需要选择最能区分这些假设的关键指标：C1 消融在均匀分配下的困惑度（待进行的实验）、BCE 在 30% 时的困惑度（正在运行）以及 KL 在推荐工作点的 MMLU 均值。\n\n现在我在计算 KL 和 TEAL 在 30% 分配下的 MMLU 差异，检查 KL 的三个种子平均值约为 0.385，而 TEAL 在 30% 时的准确率是 0.3861，但我需要确认这是否使用了全局分配。\n\n看起来在推荐的操作点（30%，均匀分配）上，KL 的 MMLU 性能与 TEAL 相当，甚至略低，虽然 KL 在困惑度上有明显优势（8.82 vs 10.97）。\n\n现在我需要确认几个关键的待处理结果：C1 消融在均匀分配下是否仍然成立，BCE 在 30% 时与 KL 的对比，以及其他几个重要的基准测试。\n\n这些实验将决定我们最终落在哪个层级。\n\n让我开始写最终的 YAML 配置，并明确说明这些数字的含义。我在比较不同方法在 30% 稀疏度下的表现：KL 全局分配的 PPL 是 8.98，均匀分配是 8.82，而 TEAL 是 10.97。BCE 在 50% 时的表现明显更差，所以我需要看看 30% 时的具体差距。\n\n根据 50% 时 BCE 和 KL 之间的 2.4-3 倍差异，我可以推断 30% 时 BCE 的 PPL 可能在 10 到 20+ 之间，这会直接影响我们结果的说服力。\n\n现在我想更仔细地理解 c1_ablation_eval_uniform 这个实验的含义。\n\n看起来 C1 ablation 用 BCE 训练了一个预测器来模仿 KL 的掩码决策，在约 61% 的稀疏度下达到了 9.29 的 PPL，这与 KL 在 50% 时的 9.15-9.25 相当——这意味着在更高的稀疏度下反而获得了相似的性能，这似乎有些不寻常，除非 oracle agreement 的含义有所不同。\n\n实际上，KL 预测器在其原生 60.9% 稀疏度下本身就能达到约 9.2 的 PPL，而 BCE 预测器在相同掩码上训练后达到 9.29 PPL 且有 97.8% 的一致性，这就说得通了。现在我在考虑 c1_ablation_eval_uniform 的结果，它会用统一的层级分配来评估同一个预测器。\n\n如果强制 50% 的统一稀疏度，KL 预测器的 PPL 约为 11.96，而 C1 消融预测器应该也接近这个值，假设掩码质量能够转移。如果一致性保持在 98% 左右，PPL 应该在 12.0 左右，否则可能会更差。\n\n但我意识到这个实验可能是在 30% 稀疏度下进行的，这是项目推荐的操作点，在这种情况下 KL 的 PPL 是 8.82，而 C1 消融的范围会在 8.5 到 14.0 之间，取决于掩码转移的效果。我觉得我在纠结具体数字了，还是先生成一个结构良好的 YAML 配置，用合理的范围来表示，然后继续。\n\n用户说的\"Dream 模拟\"是要我定义三个场景来规划论文策略，所以我现在在构建一个 YAML 结构，其中最好的情况是 C1 消融在统一分配下验证成功，BCE 在 30% 稀疏度下明显劣于 KL，并且掩码模式发现的叙事完全成立。\n\nGenerating YAML structure... Looking at the performance metrics across different sparsity levels and training approaches, the core narrative is becoming clear: the mask patterns themselves are what drive the quality improvements, not the training objective. KL distillation discovers activation masks that diverge significantly from magnitude-based heuristics, and when BCE is trained on these same mask targets, it recovers the KL performance—demonstrating that the masks matter more than the loss function. The paper's main contribution stands firm, and I can address reviewer concerns through iso-compute comparisons, the ablation study, and Pareto frontier analysis.\n\nHowever, the ablation results suggest I need to be more measured in my claims. The oracle agreement on mask transfer sits around 85-95%, with a 5-15% PPL gap compared to KL, indicating the masks transfer effectively but with some loss. BCE trained at 30% sparsity reaches PPL around 9.5-12.0, which is worse than KL but comparable to the baseline, and the mask divergence shrinks as sparsity increases, meaning KL's advantage becomes less pronounced at higher compression levels. The trends across the 25-35% sweep are directionally correct, though there's some noise in the regime boundaries. 我需要调整第一个贡献的叙述方式，从强调\"掩码就是一切\"转向\"掩码是主导因素，但损失函数也有残余影响\"。同时加强第三个贡献作为独立分析，通过增加25%和35%的数据点来提高数据密度，即使结果不够尖锐。还要扩展下游任务的覆盖范围，加入HellaSwag、PIQA和BoolQ。EMNLP主会仍然可能，但需要更精细的定位和更多消融实验来支撑论文。\n\n最坏情况下，如果C1消融失败或BCE在30%时接近KL的性能，那么掩码模式发现的故事就会崩塌，需要重新定位整篇论文。关键的失败指标包括：C1消融的oracle一致性低于0.85或与均匀PPL的差距超过15%，这意味着掩码质量无法独立于KL损失来解释性能优势；BCE在30%时的TEAL困惑度接近9.5以下，说明标准BCE在推荐操作点已经接近KL的效果；掩码散度在低稀疏度时KL和幅度掩码趋同；以及25%到35%的扫描显示单调退化而无转折点。\n\n现在我在查看25%稀疏度下的困惑度范围与30%几乎无差异，以及掩码散度的具体数值，这进一步支持了放弃掩码模式发现作为核心主张的决定。我需要将论文重新定位为\"输出分布蒸馏何时有助于稀疏推理？一项综合分析\"，通过系统性地分析损失函数、分配策略和稀疏度的三维交互，覆盖4种损失、2种分配方式、5个稀疏度水平和3个模型规模，虽然KL的困惑度优势在不同规模和稀疏度上保持稳健，但下游任务的优势仅在高稀疏度时出现。\n\n补偿网络被证明不必要，而困惑度与下游任务的脱节随规模变化，这使得论文从发现型转变为分析型，为从业者提供选择SwiGLU模型损失函数、分配策略和稀疏度的指南。现在我在验证不同层级之间的范围一致性，确保\"好\"指标遵循最佳≥中位数≥最差的顺序。 I'm finishing up the consistency checks on the remaining metrics—they all look good with the ranges properly ordered. Now I need to verify whether these ranges actually make sense given the data I've already collected, starting with the 30% uniform perplexity results.\n\nAt 30% sparsity the C1 ablation should perform better than at 61% since there's less aggressive pruning, so a range of 8.5-9.5 for the best case seems reasonable. The median range of 9.5-11.0 accounts for some transfer loss when switching from native to uniform allocation, and the worst case of 11.0-16.0 covers substantial failures. But I'm noticing something—if the C1 ablation already achieved 97.8% oracle agreement at native sparsity, would the uniform allocation really drop it below 85%? The uniform allocation only changes the distribution of pruned neurons per layer, not the predictor itself, so the scores should still align fairly closely with KL's performance.\n\nActually, the key issue is that the predictor was trained on KL's global top-k masks, so when I apply uniform per-layer budgets instead, the selected neurons differ and oracle agreement naturally drops. If I'm just evaluating the existing model under uniform allocation rather than retraining, the agreement could definitely decline. Looking at the KL baseline at 30% across different seeds, the PPL stays fairly stable around 8.82-8.96, though MMLU shows more variance, which suggests my ranges are reasonable.\n\nBut now I'm checking the actual MMLU numbers for uniform allocation—the paper claims variance drops from 9.5pp to 1.3pp, but when I calculate from the data I have (s42: 0.4061, s123: 0.3763, s456: 0.3727), I'm getting a standard deviation around 1.87pp, which doesn't quite match that claimed improvement. Let me verify what the global allocation numbers actually show. values are:\n- s42 global: 0.2874 (from lm_eval_kl_30pct_s42)\n- s123 global: 0.3807 (from kl_30pct_s123)  \n- s456 global: 0.4382 (from kl30_s456_global_eval)\n\nMean = 0.3688, std = 0.0765 = 7.65pp\n\nSo global std = 7.65pp, uniform std = 1.87pp. Ratio = 4.1x (paper says 7.5x but that might use different seeds or earlier data).\n\nAnyway, the point is that at 30% uniform, MMLU is ~38.5% on average. Dense is 65.3%. That's still a -26.8pp gap.\n\nFor the best tier, I initially said [0.40, 0.45], and the best seed so far is 0.4061. But realistically, pushing beyond 0.43 seems unlikely with the current setup—maybe [0.38, 0.43] is more honest. The real question is whether we're defining \"best\" as what additional optimization could theoretically achieve versus what we actually expect from the pending experiments. Given the three existing seeds cluster around 37-41%, the mean sits around 38.5%, so I should probably calibrate the range more conservatively. , 0.36]\n\nThese ranges are tighter and more grounded in what we've actually observed. But I'm realizing these are existing data points—the tier distinction should really hinge on what the pending experiments will show us. Since we already have three seeds, the mean is pretty much locked in around 38.5%, so MMLU might not be the right lever for differentiating tiers anyway.\n\nI should focus on the experiments we're actually running: whether the mask quality story holds under uniform allocation, whether KL dominates BCE at 30%, and what the regime boundaries look like at different sparsity levels. Those are the outcomes that will actually shift our confidence in the tiers.\n\nI'm going to keep MMLU as a metric but treat it as mostly known, with the understanding that any additional downstream evaluations might nudge the picture. Really, I should stop overthinking the statistical framing and just produce something practical and actionable for the user.\n\nMy narrative directions are solid: best case is the original mask pattern discovery framing works as planned, median is softening the claims with more evidence while staying viable for EMNLP Main, and worst case is reframing as empirical analysis rather than discovery but still targeting the same venue. That's a useful structure to work from.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":33900,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":33900},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b20fdd82-07fe-4ec0-a09f-ffbdb7bb4357","uuid":"ecc32836-9304-400c-999f-0200599ea78f"}
